A||||5||Doni Hagan||O2 sensor blown off! How to remove stub?||||1||
Z||000000||Doni Hagan||12-08-2001||11:08 AM||||A friend's O2 sensor is blown out in the center and I've read threads about breaking the pipe while trying to remove the nut. Is there any suggestion about the best way to do this without further damage? Should the pipe be hot? Soak in penetrating oil? WHAT?<P>e-mail me with some info....I don't check into this part of the Forum that often. <P>Thanks. ||||reg||1
Z||000001||ka4nkf||12-09-2001||08:36 PM||||On the O2 sensor, I would try lots of pentrating oil first, then If It does not come out without a lot of force, then you will have to heat It up with a torch almost red and screw It out. good luck<BR>Don||||reg||1
Z||000002||Jake_Dragon||12-09-2001||09:08 PM||||I drove around the block a few times and used a 1/2 drive socket and cheater.<BR>Came right out, only required 2 bad words.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||12-09-2001||11:53 PM||||Let's put it this way.... if the bung breaks loose then it was F'd anyway.<P>keep in mind, heating the bung red hot alters the metal. Usually weakening it. Avoid that if you can.<P>I'm guessing you have a sensor that blew out of it's shell. The shells can be very brittle. Usually the shell will break before the bung will.<P>Try the penetrating oil. even try t running it after shoving something in the hole to stop a huge leak. (The manifold suold expand every so slightly more than the sensor shell. don't drive like that. don't forget to reset the ECM even if there are no codes!)<P>These thin manifolds are very prone to cracking if heated/cooled unevenly. try to avoid a torch if you can.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Jhonny||12-10-2001||10:17 AM||||When I replaced my 02 sensor after it fell apart it came out with no major problems.<BR>Just drive the car for a 10-15 minutes so that the manifold gets warm.<BR>Don't forget to smear copper paste on threads of the new 02 sensor to avoid future problems.<P>/// Jhonny<BR><P>------------------<BR>Jhonny Sokura<BR>Vadstena, Östergötland Sweden<P><A HREF="http://home.swipnet.se/jks/fiero3.jpg" TARGET=_blank> Pontiac Fiero GT -87 , V6 5spd<BR> </A>||||reg||1
Z||000005||GTDude||12-15-2001||10:13 PM||||use a good penetrating oil....my favorite is PB Blaster...better than any i've used.  If the nut is still good from the o2 sensor, use a 7/8 short socket and it should come right out.  I dont' recomend heating it.  If it is really tight or the nut from the sensor is messed up, use a chisel and hammer and chisel around the nut....not just in one spot to get it to loosen.  This does two things.  The pounding loosens the rust and the chiseling twists the nut in the right direction.  Never had this method fail.  Wish you luck.<P>Phil Pettus<P>------------------<BR>GTDude's 86GT||||reg||1
